# Echoing Images in PHP: Why `src` Needs Absolute Paths

When embedding an image directly into a view rendered by PHP, the most common pitfall is constructing the `<img>` tag with a relative path when the browser expects an absolute one. The code snippet provided attempts to display an image located at `/Applications/MAMP/htdocs/php_test/image_archive/`, but it fails because of how the `src` attribute resolves paths in the context of a web server.

The specific line causing the issue is:
```php
echo '<img src="' . $file_path[0] . '" alt="error">';
```

While `$file_path[0]` contains the string `/Applications/MAMP/htdocs/php_test/image_archive/...`, this path is an **absolute filesystem path**, not a web-accessible URL. When your browser receives the HTML, it attempts to fetch `http://localhost/Applications/MAMP/htdocs/php_test/image_archive/...`. Web servers like Apache or Nginx generally do not serve files from outside their configured document root unless explicitly told to. Even if they did, the resulting URL would be malformed and inaccessible via a standard browser address bar.

To resolve this, you must transform the filesystem path into a web URL. In PHP, the built-in `$_SERVER['DOCUMENT_ROOT']` variable holds the path where your web server is configured to look for files. By concatenating `$file_path[0]` with this variable, or by using `dirname()` and `basename()` logic depending on your specific need, you can ensure the path starts with your domain root rather than the OS drive letter.

Consider how a robust solution would construct the URL dynamically:

```php
// Assuming $file_path[0] is just the relative filename like 'logo.png'
$relativePath = basename($file_path[0]); 

// Construct the web-safe URL
$url = dirname($_SERVER['DOCUMENT_ROOT']) . '/' . $relativePath;

echo '<img src="' . htmlspecialchars($url) . '" alt="error">';
```

However, in your specific scenario, `$file_path[0]` seems to be intended as a full path. If you are on a local environment like MAMP, the document root is often `/Applications/MAMP/htdocs`. If your server is configured correctly, you can simply strip the leading slash and prepend `$_SERVER['DOCUMENT_ROOT']`:

```php
$webPath = str_replace($_SERVER['DOCUMENT_ROOT'], '', $file_path[0]);
// This results in a relative path like /image_archive/file.jpg
echo '<img src="' . $_SERVER['DOCUMENT_ROOT'] . $webPath . '" alt="error">';
```

The critical takeaway is that the `src` attribute must point to a location the browser can reach via HTTP. If you are developing on MAMP, simply using the raw filesystem path in the `src` attribute will never work because the browser cannot navigate from `localhost:8888` into `/Applications/`.

Security considerations also come into play when echoing HTML containing user data or file paths. Always use `htmlspecialchars()` on the value assigned to `src` (and `alt`) before outputting it. This prevents Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if the `$last_file[0]` variable contains malicious script injection attempts disguised as filenames.

If you are moving away from raw PHP logic into a framework like Laravel, the approach shifts slightly. Frameworks handle path resolution for you via helper functions or public asset directories (`public/`). In that ecosystem, you would typically place images in `storage/app/public` and symlink them to the `public/storage` folder, then reference them using `asset()` helpers or direct URLs relative to the public root. For more details on handling assets and paths in modern PHP stacks, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) offer guidance on best practices for file management and serving static content securely.

Ultimately, debugging image display issues often boils down to one question: Is the URL inside the `src` attribute accessible via a web browser? If you paste the path into the address bar of your browser and it doesn't load, PHP has served the correct HTML, but the browser is looking in the wrong place. Adjusting the path construction to respect the document root resolves this immediately.
